FINDING DETAIL: In the curved branch the codebook-side contract term `F.mse_loss(x, x_q.detach())` is kept although `x_q` is detached and the codebook has no gradient; the term is a constant that only inflates the reported loss and the `torch.isfinite(loss)` check. It changes no gradient and no SID, hence minor. (Related hardening, also non-blocking: `init_codebook` at layers.py:198-201 queries the ball codebook with the raw tangent residual `x` instead of `exp_0(x)`, so the first-level-residual assignment used for initialising levels 2-3 is made in mixed coordinates; it affects only the KMeans seed of the last two levels.)
FINDING REPRODUCTION: layers.py:174-178 — `x_q = log_0(...)`, then `F.mse_loss(x, x_q.detach())`; `x` is the input batch (requires_grad=False), so `.grad_fn` of this term is None and it contributes 0 to `loss.backward()` while still being added to `loss` at model/model.py:94.
FINDING CORRECTION: Drop the constant term in the curved branch (keep `beta*(weights*commitment).mean()`), or use it only for logging; and feed `exp_0(x)` to `frechet.distances` in `init_codebook` so the initial assignment uses the same geometry as training.

